  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This SARS-CoV-2 forecasting preprint has begun to attract scholarly attention: OpenAlex records 5 citations, and the reuse record shows 7 downstream uses, including later articles, preprints, and Europe PMC article mentions. The strongest signal is not broad public attention but early methodological uptake, with subsequent work in the same forecasting and fitness-modeling space building on its approach. The article also moved from preprint to a peer-reviewed publication, indicating that the underlying study was successfully carried forward.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Abousamra, Figgins, and Bedford\u2019s study on short-term forecasts of SARS-CoV-2 variant frequency enters a fast-moving area of genomic epidemiology, where the practical value of a model is judged by whether it can track variant turnover in time to inform surveillance. The paper compares forecasting frameworks for variant growth and fitness, positioning multivariate and growth-advantage formulations as tools for anticipating changes in lineage frequency. In the record available here, the work shows an early but meaningful scholarly footprint rather than a broad public one.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest sign of influence is downstream reuse. OpenAlex captures 5 citations, and the reuse graph adds 7 documented instances of reuse, including later articles, preprints, and two Europe PMC article mentions. The citing literature is concentrated in 2024 and 2025 and is strongly aligned with SARS-CoV-2 frequency forecasting, fitness estimation, and related evolutionary analysis. That pattern suggests the paper is being read primarily as a methodological contribution within a focused research community.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing landscape also points to intellectual continuity in this niche. Subsequent works include a Nature article on spike deep mutational scanning and clade success, as well as later preprint and journal work on lineage frequency forecasting and transmission fitness estimation. These are not generic references to COVID-19, but follow-on studies in the same analytic space, which is a stronger indication of methodological relevance than broad diffusion alone.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The broader attention record is comparatively quiet: event-data and public-discourse sources are unavailable here, so there is no documented evidence in this digest of news or social-media reach. Even so, the paper\u2019s move from medRxiv to a peer-reviewed publication, together with its early citation and reuse trail, indicates that it has already become part of the technical conversation around SARS-CoV-2 variant forecasting.</p>",
